menu "Station: diagnostics"

    config DIAG_LOG_RING_KB
        int "Log ring for the web UI (KB per boot, PSRAM, kept across resets)"
        range 8 1024
        default 64
        help
            Every log line is also copied here so it can be read remotely
            (web UI log view, WebSocket stream). The ring survives software,
            panic and watchdog resets, so the previous boot's last lines can
            be read after a crash: two rings of this size are kept in PSRAM,
            this boot's and the previous boot's.

    config DIAG_LOG_LINE_MAX
        int "Longest log line kept (bytes)"
        range 128 1024
        default 256
        help
            Each line is formatted on the stack of the task that logs, so
            this adds to every logging task's stack use. Longer lines are
            cut and end in "..." on the console and in the log ring.

    config DIAG_CONSOLE_TX_BUF
        int "Console transmit buffer (bytes, internal RAM)"
        range 1024 32768
        default 4096
        help
            The serial console runs at 115200 baud (about 11.5 KB/s). A log
            line goes to the console only when it fits in this buffer, so a
            burst never stalls the task that logs; lines that do not fit are
            counted as dropped (the log ring still has them) and the console
            says how many before its output resumes.

    config DIAG_JOURNAL_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Minimum interval between journal records of one kind (ms)"
        range 0 60000
        default 1000
        help
            Rate limit that keeps a flapping condition from wearing out the
            journal partition. A record it refuses is reported as not
            written, so callers that accumulate deltas keep them pending.
            Health-ladder step records are exempt: one step's result and the
            next step's start fall in the same second, and the ladder bounds
            their rate itself (each step has a grace of at least 5 s).

    config DIAG_JOURNAL_BUDGET_PER_H
        int "Journal records per hour, all kinds together"
        range 60 36000
        default 1800
        help
            Second rate limit, over all kinds: at most this many records per
            hour on average, of which up to ten minutes' worth may come at
            once. Refused records are reported like the per-kind limit's;
            ladder step records are exempt. Every 127 records cost one
            sector erase: at the default, a journal written at the full rate
            without a break would take more than 20 years to reach 100 000
            erases per sector.

endmenu
